                  },
                  "displayName" : "Lewis Pringle",
                  "self" : "https://stroika.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=557058%3A4fe560ca-b4e1-41d1-9bdf-97b1684eda32",
                  "timeZone" : "America/New_York"
               },
               "body" : {
                  "content" : [
                     {
                        "content" : [
                           {
                              "text" : "Some ideas on how to do this:",
                              "type" : "text"
                           }
                        ],
                        "type" : "paragraph"
                     },
                     {
                        "content" : [
                           {
                              "text" : "o Could store each char* (char32_t* etc) values in the envelope, so can see the const/non-const of the methods. Non-const methods would then FREE the extra pointers and follow C++ safety rules.",
                              "type" : "text"
                           }
                        ],
                        "type" : "paragraph"
                     },
                     {
                        "content" : [
                           {
                              "text" : "o Maybe better - store char8, char16, and char32* pointers in REP itself. Make it impl detail which is stores as 'base copy'.  MAYBE use one big buffer for all these strings, and just have pointers that point into it? For small strings this would work well. For larger, they would need to be malloced. Because the c_str() style methods are all const, this means we'd need a LOCK around read/write these cached pointers (costly). Probably a single global lock - otherwise might be too big? Or maybe SpinLock (small and fast).",
                              "type" : "text"
                           }
                        ],
                        "type" : "paragraph"
                     }
                  ],
                  "type" : "doc",
                  "version" : 1
               },
